(function(){"use strict";window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES=window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES||{};window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES["l2"]={"id":"l2","lectureNumber":2,"lectureLabel":"Lecture 2","title":"What is Globalization? Understanding the International System in an Era of Global Disruption","theme":"Theme 1 — Understanding the Contemporary World","bankVersion":"tu101-l2-v13.16","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summary":[{"id":"tu2s1","titleEn":"1. Globalization — More Than Trade","titleTh":"1. Globalization — มากกว่าการค้าระหว่างประเทศ","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Interdependence across borders.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: การพึ่งพากันข้ามพรมแดน","bullets":[{"en":"<b>Globalization</b> is growing interdependence among economies, cultures and populations through trade, investment, technology flows and exchange of ideas.","th":"<b>Globalization</b> คือการพึ่งพากันมากขึ้นของเศรษฐกิจ วัฒนธรรม และประชากร ผ่านการค้า การลงทุน เทคโนโลยี และการแลกเปลี่ยนความคิด"},{"en":"The lecture uses four dimensions: <b>economic, political, cultural and technological</b>.","th":"สไลด์แบ่งเป็น 4 มิติ: <b>เศรษฐกิจ การเมือง วัฒนธรรม และเทคโนโลยี</b>"},{"en":"The key exam trap is reducing globalization to imports and exports. It also changes authority, communication, identity and political networks.","th":"ข้อหลอกคือคิดว่า globalization = การค้าอย่างเดียว ทั้งที่ยังเปลี่ยนอำนาจ การสื่อสาร อัตลักษณ์ และเครือข่ายการเมือง"}],"understandEn":"Globalization describes cross-border connections becoming dense enough that events, decisions and production in one place increasingly affect people elsewhere.","understandTh":"ให้คิดว่าโลกเชื่อมกันหนาแน่นขึ้น จนการตัดสินใจ เหตุการณ์ และการผลิตในที่หนึ่งส่งผลต่อผู้คนอีกที่ได้มากขึ้น","diagram":["Trade & investment","Technology","Ideas & culture","Political networks"]},{"id":"tu2s2","titleEn":"2. Waves of Globalization","titleTh":"2. คลื่นของ Globalization","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Ancient → Industrial → Reversal → Post-war → Digital.","memoryTh":"จำลำดับ: โบราณ → อุตสาหกรรม → ถอยกลับ → หลังสงคราม → ดิจิทัล","bullets":[{"en":"The slide traces <b>ancient trade</b>, an <b>industrial wave</b> in the 1800s–1914, a <b>reversal</b> in 1914–1945, <b>post-war expansion</b>, and the <b>digital era</b>.","th":"สไลด์ไล่จากการค้าโบราณ → คลื่นอุตสาหกรรม 1800s–1914 → ช่วงถอยกลับ 1914–1945 → การขยายตัวหลังสงคราม → ยุคดิจิทัล"},{"en":"The WTO reading reinforces the role of transport and communication technologies in reducing distance and trade costs.","th":"บทอ่าน WTO ย้ำบทบาทของเทคโนโลยีขนส่งและสื่อสารที่ลดต้นทุนของระยะทางและการค้า"},{"en":"Global integration can expand and contract; it is not a one-way historical process.","th":"การเชื่อมโยงโลกสามารถขยายและหดตัวได้ ไม่ใช่กระบวนการทางเดียว"}],"understandEn":"Globalization is old, but its intensity and mechanisms change. Wars and political shocks can reverse integration; technology and supportive political orders can accelerate it.","understandTh":"Globalization มีมานาน แต่ความเข้มข้นและกลไกเปลี่ยนไป สงคราม/การเมืองทำให้ถอยกลับได้ ขณะที่เทคโนโลยีและระเบียบการเมืองที่เอื้อช่วยเร่งได้","diagram":["Ancient trade","Industrial wave","1914–45 reversal","Post-war","Digital era"]},{"id":"tu2s3","titleEn":"3. Why Post-Cold War Globalization Accelerated","titleTh":"3. ทำไม Globalization หลังสงครามเย็นจึงเร่งขึ้น","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Technology + Policy + Ideology.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: Technology + Policy + Ideology","bullets":[{"en":"After the Cold War, ideological bloc confrontation ended and the <b>United States became the sole superpower</b>.","th":"หลังสงครามเย็น การเผชิญหน้าระหว่างสองค่ายลดลง และ <b>สหรัฐฯ กลายเป็นมหาอำนาจหลักเพียงขั้วเดียว</b>"},{"en":"The lecture links this environment to democracy, the <b>Liberal International Order</b>, economic liberalization and regional integration.","th":"สไลด์เชื่อมกับประชาธิปไตย <b>Liberal International Order</b> การเปิดเสรีเศรษฐกิจ และ regional integration"},{"en":"Three drivers accelerated globalization: <b>technology</b>, government <b>policy choices</b> that lowered barriers, and <b>ideology</b> favoring free markets.","th":"ตัวขับ 3 อย่างคือ <b>เทคโนโลยี</b> <b>นโยบาย</b> ลดกำแพงการค้า และ <b>อุดมการณ์</b> ที่เชื่อในตลาดเสรี"}],"understandEn":"The lecture treats globalization as politically enabled, not technologically automatic. Technology creates possibilities; governments and ideas determine how open the system becomes.","understandTh":"เทคโนโลยีไม่ได้ทำให้ globalization เกิดเองทั้งหมด เทคโนโลยีสร้างความเป็นไปได้ แต่นโยบายรัฐและความคิดเป็นตัวกำหนดว่าโลกจะเปิดแค่ไหน","diagram":["Technology","Policy choices","Free-market ideology","Faster integration"]},{"id":"tu2s4","titleEn":"4. Political Effects — Sovereignty, Actors & Governance","titleTh":"4. ผลทางการเมือง — อธิปไตย ผู้เล่น และ Governance","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: States still matter, but they are no longer alone.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: รัฐยังสำคัญ แต่ไม่ใช่ผู้เล่นเพียงกลุ่มเดียว","bullets":[{"en":"Power increasingly involves states plus <b>multinational corporations, NGOs, international institutions and social movements</b>.","th":"อำนาจเกี่ยวข้องทั้งรัฐและ <b>บริษัทข้ามชาติ NGOs สถาบันระหว่างประเทศ และ social movements</b>"},{"en":"Decision-making can occur through transnational networks, challenging the idea that authority stays entirely inside national borders.","th":"การตัดสินใจบางส่วนเกิดผ่านเครือข่ายข้ามชาติ จึงท้าทายแนวคิดว่าอำนาจอยู่ภายในพรมแดนรัฐทั้งหมด"},{"en":"Examples of global governance include the <b>UN, IMF, World Bank, WTO</b> and regional organizations such as ASEAN.","th":"ตัวอย่าง global governance ได้แก่ <b>UN, IMF, World Bank, WTO</b> และองค์กรภูมิภาคอย่าง ASEAN"},{"en":"The rise of China, India and other actors changes the distribution of power and creates pressure on the existing liberal order.","th":"การเติบโตของจีน อินเดีย และผู้เล่นอื่นเปลี่ยนการกระจายอำนาจและเพิ่มแรงกดดันต่อระเบียบเสรีเดิม"}],"understandEn":"Sovereignty is challenged, not erased. States negotiate and share parts of rule-making with institutions and non-state actors while remaining central political units.","understandTh":"อธิปไตยถูกท้าทาย ไม่ได้หายไป รัฐยังเป็นหน่วยหลัก แต่ต้องเจรจาและแบ่งพื้นที่การกำหนดกติกากับสถาบันและผู้เล่นที่ไม่ใช่รัฐ","diagram":["State","MNC / NGO","International institutions","Transnational governance"]},{"id":"tu2s5","titleEn":"5. Economic Gains — Why Integration Can Be Attractive","titleTh":"5. ผลประโยชน์ทางเศรษฐกิจ — ทำไมการเชื่อมโลกจึงน่าดึงดูด","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Specialize, connect, spread, scale.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: เชี่ยวชาญ → เชื่อมโยง → กระจายเทคโนโลยี → ประสิทธิภาพ","bullets":[{"en":"<b>Comparative advantage</b> allows specialization and trade, which can lower prices and increase variety.","th":"<b>Comparative advantage</b> ทำให้เกิดการแบ่งความเชี่ยวชาญและการค้า ช่วยลดราคาและเพิ่มทางเลือก"},{"en":"The lecture highlights global poverty reduction, innovation diffusion and integrated supply chains as major gains.","th":"สไลด์เน้นการลดความยากจน การกระจายนวัตกรรม และ supply chain ระหว่างประเทศเป็นผลดีสำคัญ"},{"en":"The WTO reading shows post-1945 trade, FDI and multinational production expanding alongside falling trade/communication costs.","th":"บทอ่าน WTO แสดงว่าหลัง 1945 การค้า FDI และการผลิตของบริษัทข้ามชาติขยายตัวพร้อมกับต้นทุนการค้า/การสื่อสารที่ลดลง"}],"understandEn":"Efficiency gains are real, but they are aggregate gains. The fact that the economy gains does not tell us who receives the gains or who bears adjustment costs.","understandTh":"ประโยชน์ด้านประสิทธิภาพมีจริง แต่เป็นประโยชน์ในภาพรวม จึงยังต้องถามต่อว่าใครได้ประโยชน์ และใครต้องรับต้นทุนจากการปรับตัว","diagram":["Specialization","Trade","Supply chains","Lower cost / innovation"]},{"id":"tu2s6","titleEn":"6. Criticism, Populism & Deglobalization","titleTh":"6. คำวิจารณ์ Populism และ Deglobalization","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Global gains can create local losers.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: โลกอาจได้ แต่บางกลุ่มในประเทศอาจเสีย","bullets":[{"en":"Criticisms include <b>within-country inequality, worker displacement, labor-rights problems, environmental costs and cultural homogenization</b>.","th":"คำวิจารณ์รวมถึง <b>ความเหลื่อมล้ำในประเทศ คนงานถูกแทนที่ ปัญหาสิทธิแรงงาน ผลกระทบสิ่งแวดล้อม และวัฒนธรรมถูกทำให้คล้ายกัน</b>"},{"en":"A <b>democratic deficit</b> can appear when important rules are shaped by distant institutions or negotiators with limited direct public control.","th":"<b>Democratic deficit</b> เกิดได้เมื่อกติกาสำคัญถูกกำหนดโดยสถาบันหรือผู้เจรจาที่ประชาชนควบคุมโดยตรงได้น้อย"},{"en":"Economic crises, inequality, populism and geopolitical rivalry can create pressure for <b>deglobalization</b>, protectionism or supply-chain reorganization.","th":"วิกฤตเศรษฐกิจ ความเหลื่อมล้ำ populism และการแข่งขันภูมิรัฐศาสตร์ผลักดัน <b>deglobalization</b> protectionism หรือการจัด supply chain ใหม่"},{"en":"The lecture ends with a distribution question: how can benefits be kept while workers, environment, democracy and cultural diversity are protected?","th":"คำถามปลายทางคือ จะรักษาผลดีของ globalization พร้อมกับคุ้มครองแรงงาน สิ่งแวดล้อม ประชาธิปไตย และความหลากหลายทางวัฒนธรรมได้อย่างไร"}],"understandEn":"Deglobalization does not necessarily mean all cross-border connections disappear. It can mean selective decoupling, protection, reshoring or reduced dependence in strategically sensitive areas.","understandTh":"Deglobalization ไม่ได้แปลว่าการเชื่อมโลกหายหมด อาจหมายถึงการลดการพึ่งพาเฉพาะด้าน การย้ายการผลิตกลับ การตั้งกำแพง หรือแยกห่วงโซ่ในภาคยุทธศาสตร์","diagram":["Inequality / crisis","Populism","Geopolitics","Deglobalization pressure"]},{"id":"tu2s7","titleEn":"7. Power Transition — When Globalization Meets Rivalry","titleTh":"7. Power Transition — เมื่อ Globalization เจอกับการแข่งขันมหาอำนาจ","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Rising power + dissatisfied order = tension.","memoryTh":"จำสั้น ๆ: มหาอำนาจขาขึ้น + ไม่พอใจระเบียบเดิม = ความตึงเครียด","bullets":[{"en":"<b>Power Transition Theory</b> describes a hierarchical system of dominant, great, middle and small powers.","th":"<b>Power Transition Theory</b> มองระบบโลกเป็นลำดับชั้นของ dominant, great, middle และ small powers"},{"en":"A rising great power may become dissatisfied with the status quo and challenge the dominant power.","th":"มหาอำนาจที่กำลังเติบโตอาจไม่พอใจกับ status quo และท้าทายมหาอำนาจเดิม"},{"en":"The slide connects this logic to U.S. concerns that China’s economic rise enables military modernization and demands for rule changes.","th":"สไลด์เชื่อมกับความกังวลของสหรัฐฯ ว่าการเติบโตเศรษฐกิจของจีนช่วยการพัฒนาทหารและเพิ่มแรงผลักให้เปลี่ยนกติกา"},{"en":"This tension connects Lecture 2 directly to Lecture 3’s U.S.–China competition and ASEAN hedging.","th":"จุดนี้เป็นสะพานตรงไป Lecture 3 เรื่องการแข่งขันสหรัฐฯ–จีนและการ hedging ของ ASEAN"}],"understandEn":"Globalization can create interdependence and prosperity while also changing relative power. Economic integration therefore does not automatically remove geopolitical rivalry.","understandTh":"Globalization สร้างการพึ่งพาและความมั่งคั่งได้ แต่ก็เปลี่ยนดุลอำนาจด้วย ดังนั้นการค้าระหว่างกันไม่ได้ทำให้การแข่งขันภูมิรัฐศาสตร์หายไปโดยอัตโนมัติ","diagram":["Economic rise","Military / political power","Status-quo challenge","Great-power tension"]}],"parts":{"tu2-foundations":{"label":"Part 1 • Meaning, Waves & Post-Cold War Order","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summaries":["tu2s1","tu2s2","tu2s3"],"topics":["What globalization means and its historical waves","Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization"]},"tu2-politics":{"label":"Part 2 • Sovereignty, Actors, Governance & Power","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summaries":["tu2s4","tu2s7"],"topics":["Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order"]},"tu2-benefits":{"label":"Part 3 • Economic Benefits & Global Production","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summaries":["tu2s5"],"topics":["Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization"]},"tu2-challenges":{"label":"Part 4 • Inequality, Criticism & Deglobalization","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summaries":["tu2s6"],"topics":["Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts"]},"tu2-all":{"label":"Cumulative • Lecture 2","source":"Professor Pongphisoot globalization slide (primary) + assigned WTO/trade reading (support)","summaries":["tu2s1","tu2s2","tu2s3","tu2s4","tu2s5","tu2s6","tu2s7"],"topics":null,"cumulative":true}},"review":{"tu2-foundations":[{"q":"Which definition best matches globalization in the lecture?","opts":["Growing interdependence of economies, cultures and populations through cross-border flows","Only an increase in exports between states","The replacement of states by international organizations","A policy of closing domestic markets"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The slide defines globalization broadly through trade, technology, investment and ideas."},{"q":"Which set contains the four globalization dimensions emphasized in the slide?","opts":["Economic, political, cultural and technological","Military, agricultural, demographic and legal","Local, provincial, national and imperial","Fiscal, monetary, electoral and judicial"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The opening framework uses economic, political, cultural and technological globalization."},{"q":"What is the correct broad sequence of globalization waves in the slide?","opts":["Ancient trade → industrial wave → 1914–45 reversal → post-war → digital era","Digital → ancient → post-war → industrial → reversal","Industrial → ancient → digital → reversal → post-war","Ancient → post-war → industrial → digital → reversal"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The slide explicitly presents this chronological sequence."},{"q":"Why is the Industrial Revolution important to the WTO reading on trade?","opts":["Transport and communication advances sharply reduced the costs of connecting markets","It ended all trade barriers permanently","It replaced manufactured trade with agriculture","It eliminated the role of politics in trade"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"Steamships, railways, telegraph and later technologies reduced distance costs."},{"q":"What major structural change followed the Cold War in the lecture?","opts":["The United States emerged as the sole superpower after bloc confrontation ended","China immediately became the sole superpower","Regional organizations disappeared","Economic liberalization was broadly rejected"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The post-Cold War slide highlights the end of bloc confrontation and U.S. primacy."},{"q":"What does Liberal International Order refer to in this lecture context?","opts":["A U.S.-supported order emphasizing liberal rules, institutions and open economic relations","A system banning regional organizations","A return to colonial empires","A purely military alliance with no economic dimension"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The slide connects U.S. primacy with democracy, institutions and economic liberalization."},{"q":"What regional trend accompanied post-Cold War globalization?","opts":["Regionalism and regional integration expanded","All regional institutions were dissolved","Countries stopped signing economic agreements","Only bilateral politics remained"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Regionalism/regional integration is listed as an emerging post-Cold War trend."},{"q":"Which is one of the three acceleration drivers named by the professor?","opts":["Technology that enables real-time cross-border coordination","Permanent elimination of national governments","Declining literacy","A ban on foreign investment"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"Technology is one of Technology + Policy Choices + Ideology."},{"q":"What policy choice helped accelerate globalization?","opts":["Lowering trade barriers and signing agreements","Closing borders to investment","Banning international institutions","Replacing markets with autarky"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"Government choices to lower barriers are one of the three drivers."},{"q":"What ideological belief supported globalization in the lecture?","opts":["Free markets were expected to support prosperity and peace","Self-sufficiency always maximizes growth","International trade inevitably causes war","Political borders should disappear completely"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"The slide lists pro-market ideology as the third acceleration driver."}],"tu2-politics":[{"q":"How does globalization challenge nation-state sovereignty in the slide?","opts":["Important decisions increasingly involve transnational actors and negotiated rules beyond borders","States legally cease to exist","Every policy is decided by corporations","National borders become physically invisible"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"The slide contrasts state-only authority with governments plus global actors/networks."},{"q":"Which is a new global actor emphasized by the lecture?","opts":["Multinational corporations","Only hereditary monarchs","Village councils only","Domestic courts exclusively"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"MNCs, NGOs, institutions and social movements are named as global actors."},{"q":"Why are NGOs important in the globalization lecture?","opts":["They can participate in transnational advocacy and governance alongside states","They replace all national elections","They issue sovereign currency","They command national armies by definition"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"NGOs are part of the expanded set of global political actors."},{"q":"Which organization is associated with global trade rules in the slide?","opts":["WTO","NATO alone","OPEC alone","Interpol alone"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"The lecture lists WTO as setting rules for global trade."},{"q":"What power shift does the slide emphasize?","opts":["The relative economic weight of the U.S./Europe declined while China and other powers rose","China and India disappeared from world trade","The U.S. share rose to nearly all world output","Power distribution stayed unchanged since 1950"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"The slide contrasts older Western dominance with a more distributed contemporary economy."},{"q":"What is the core claim of Power Transition Theory in the slide?","opts":["A rising great power may challenge a dominant power, creating systemic tension","All middle powers inevitably become hegemons","Interdependence guarantees permanent peace","Small powers control the hierarchy"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The professor links hegemonic challenge to rising dissatisfied great powers."},{"q":"Which hierarchy appears in the Power Transition Theory slide?","opts":["Dominant, great, middle and small powers","Democratic, socialist, colonial and stateless","Northern, southern, eastern and western powers","Rich, poor, urban and rural powers"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The professor presents four power categories in a hierarchical international system."},{"q":"What U.S. concern about China is highlighted in the slide?","opts":["Economic growth can support military modernization and demands to revise rules","China is expected to abandon technology","China will become economically isolated automatically","China cannot translate wealth into influence"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"The slide links economic rise, military modernization and possible challenge to the U.S.-led order."},{"q":"What was a core aim of U.S. post-Cold War grand strategy described in the slide?","opts":["Preserve U.S. leading status and prevent hostile domination of key regions","Withdraw permanently from East Asia","Transfer all security responsibility to the UN","End alliances with every state"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The slide notes preserving primacy in Europe, East Asia and the Persian Gulf."},{"q":"Does economic interdependence automatically eliminate geopolitical competition in the lecture?","opts":["No; integration can coexist with power rivalry and security concerns","Yes, trade makes conflict impossible","Yes, only poor states compete strategically","No, because globalization means there is no trade"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The lecture moves from globalization directly into power transition and U.S.–China rivalry."}],"tu2-benefits":[{"q":"What economic idea explains gains from specialization and trade in the slide?","opts":["Comparative advantage","Autarky","Mercantilist isolation","Absolute political sovereignty"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The professor presents specialization and trade as a source of lower cost and variety."},{"q":"What consumer benefit does the lecture associate with global competition?","opts":["Lower prices and greater variety","Guaranteed equal wages across countries","No need for domestic firms","Permanent elimination of inflation"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Lower prices/greater variety is Benefit 1 on the slide."},{"q":"Which broad global outcome is presented as a benefit of globalization?","opts":["Large reductions in extreme poverty, especially in parts of Asia","Universal elimination of inequality","The end of all unemployment","Equal income in every country"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The slide highlights substantial global poverty reduction."},{"q":"How can globalization spread innovation?","opts":["Ideas and technologies diffuse rapidly through cross-border networks and markets","By banning foreign technologies","By preventing firms from learning abroad","By keeping inventions inside one country"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Technology diffusion is a named benefit."},{"q":"What is an integrated global supply chain?","opts":["Production stages for one final product are distributed across multiple countries","Every product is made fully in one village","Only raw materials cross borders","A government produces all components itself"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The slide uses a modern car as a global product assembled from specialized inputs."},{"q":"What post-1945 development did the WTO reading emphasize alongside trade growth?","opts":["Rapid expansion of foreign direct investment and multinational production","Disappearance of multinational firms","End of cross-border ownership","Decline of manufactured trade to near zero"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"FDI and multinational corporations became central to global supply chains."},{"q":"What happened to many traditional tariffs during modern trade liberalization?","opts":["Average tariff barriers generally fell through unilateral and negotiated opening","They rose everywhere to 100%","They became the only trade cost","They were replaced by a ban on services trade"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading documents significant tariff reductions."},{"q":"What is a non-tariff measure (NTM)?","opts":["A non-tariff rule or policy that affects trade, such as technical or health standards","Only an export invoice","A military alliance","A currency with no trade effect"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading discusses TBT, SPS, taxes/subsidies and other non-tariff measures."},{"q":"What made more services internationally tradable?","opts":["Sharp reductions in communication costs and digital technologies","A ban on the internet","Higher shipping weight","The disappearance of education and banking"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading cites e-banking, e-commerce, e-medicine and e-learning."},{"q":"What major change occurred in developing economies’ role in world trade?","opts":["Their share of world trade rose substantially, especially with Asian industrialization","They disappeared from manufactured exports","They returned entirely to subsistence production","Only developed economies gained export share"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading highlights the growing trade weight of developing economies."}],"tu2-challenges":[{"q":"What inequality criticism does the lecture emphasize?","opts":["Globalization can increase inequality within countries even while producing aggregate gains","All countries become equally rich","Inequality only exists between countries","Trade automatically equalizes every worker’s wage"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The slide highlights wage stagnation and displaced manufacturing communities."},{"q":"Which labor concern is listed among globalization’s criticisms?","opts":["Low wages, unsafe conditions and weak bargaining protections in some supply chains","Workers everywhere receive identical protections","Labor rights become irrelevant","Global firms cannot relocate production"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The slide cites labor/human-rights concerns and cost pressure."},{"q":"Which environmental cost is associated with globalization in the slide?","opts":["Transport emissions, pollution relocation, fast consumption and e-waste","Automatic elimination of carbon emissions","No movement of waste across borders","Universal renewable energy adoption"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The lecture lists shipping, factory relocation, fast fashion and e-waste."},{"q":"What is cultural homogenization?","opts":["Local differences can be weakened as global brands/languages/consumer patterns spread","Every culture becomes more isolated","National languages necessarily multiply","Local identity becomes legally impossible"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The slide worries about global brands, English dominance and consumer culture."},{"q":"What is the democratic-deficit criticism?","opts":["Important rules may be shaped by institutions or negotiators with weak direct accountability to ordinary citizens","Democracy requires zero international cooperation","Only elected legislatures can make any treaty","Globalization eliminates elections"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"The slide highlights limited public say over some trade/institutional decisions."},{"q":"How can inequality contribute to populism in the deglobalization story?","opts":["Groups who feel left behind may support leaders promising protection and national control","Inequality always increases support for more globalization","Populism only comes from technology","Economic grievances cannot affect politics"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The lecture connects inequality and backlash to populist politics."},{"q":"Why can economic crises slow globalization?","opts":["Crises can increase protectionism, distrust and pressure to reduce external dependence","Crises always eliminate tariffs","Crises make supply chains more stable automatically","Crises prevent governments from changing policy"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Economic crisis is named as a deglobalization factor."},{"q":"How can geopolitics drive deglobalization?","opts":["Security rivalry can motivate decoupling, restrictions and supply-chain diversification","Rivalry necessarily increases dependence on one competitor","Security has no effect on trade policy","Geopolitics only changes culture"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"World disruption and geopolitical rivalry are central deglobalization drivers."},{"q":"What does deglobalization most accurately mean in this course context?","opts":["A reduction or reorganization of cross-border integration, often selectively rather than total isolation","The complete disappearance of all international exchange","Any rise in domestic production","The creation of a regional agreement"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Deglobalization can involve fragmentation, protection and strategic restructuring."},{"q":"What central policy challenge closes the globalization balance sheet?","opts":["Keep genuine benefits while distributing them more fairly and protecting workers, environment and democracy","Maximize trade regardless of any social cost","End all international institutions","Choose either total globalization or total isolation"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The professor frames the real question as how to retain benefits while addressing costs."}]},"mastery":{"tu2-foundations":[{"q":"A country has little merchandise trade growth but rapidly expanding digital services, cross-border investment and cultural exchange. What is the best conclusion?","opts":["Globalization can still be deepening because it is multidimensional","Globalization must be declining because goods trade alone defines it","The country has left the international system","Only cultural globalization exists when merchandise trade is flat"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The lecture treats economic, technological, cultural and political links as parts of globalization."},{"q":"A social-media platform spreads music globally while firms coordinate production and activists organize across borders. Which lecture idea best unifies these changes?","opts":["Multiple dimensions of globalization operate simultaneously","Only technological globalization matters","These are examples of deglobalization","They prove state sovereignty has disappeared"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The examples combine technological, cultural, economic and political dimensions."},{"q":"Which historical lesson follows from the globalization-wave timeline?","opts":["International integration can expand, reverse and then re-expand","Globalization has increased continuously without interruption","Technology makes political shocks irrelevant","Only the digital era counts as globalization"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The 1914–45 reversal shows integration is not a one-way process."},{"q":"Containerization and broadband both support the same deeper mechanism. What is it?","opts":["Reducing the economic importance of distance and enabling cross-border specialization","Making all countries produce identical goods","Ending the need for firms to invest abroad","Replacing comparative advantage with self-sufficiency"],"ans":0,"topic":"What globalization means and its historical waves","why":"The WTO reading emphasizes the 'death of distance' through transport and communication change."},{"q":"Why did the post-Cold War environment favor globalization according to the lecture?","opts":["U.S. primacy, liberalization and wider acceptance of liberal rules created an enabling order","All states surrendered sovereignty to the UN","Military conflict became impossible","Trade no longer required policy choices"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Political order and policy choices supported integration; technology alone was insufficient."},{"q":"Which development would most directly weaken the post-Cold War liberal-order foundation described in class?","opts":["Major powers rejecting shared rules and using strategic separation instead","Faster internet connections","More regional trade agreements","Greater cross-border student exchange"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Rule rejection and strategic fragmentation undercut the political order supporting integration."},{"q":"Why can regional integration and globalization coexist rather than contradict each other?","opts":["Regional agreements can lower barriers and organize wider cross-border integration","Regions necessarily close themselves to all outsiders","Globalization requires the absence of institutions","Regionalism only concerns military affairs"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Regional bodies can be building blocks of economic and political integration."},{"q":"Faster internet exists, but a government blocks investment, raises tariffs and restricts data flows. What does the lecture predict?","opts":["Technology alone may not produce deep globalization because policy choices can restrict it","Globalization must accelerate regardless of policy","The country automatically becomes a liberal democracy","Trade barriers become irrelevant once internet speed rises"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"Globalization is enabled jointly by technology, policy and ideology."},{"q":"Two countries have identical technology, but one joins trade agreements while the other raises barriers. What variable best explains different integration levels?","opts":["Policy choices","Geography alone","Language family","Population age structure"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"Policy choices determine how much technological possibility becomes actual integration."},{"q":"Why is ideology analytically different from technology in the globalization story?","opts":["Ideology supplies beliefs about what policies are desirable, while technology supplies capabilities","Ideology measures internet speed","Technology decides whether markets are morally legitimate","They are identical concepts"],"ans":0,"topic":"Technology, policy choices, ideology, and globalization","why":"Ideas shape government choices; technology changes feasible connections."}],"tu2-politics":[{"q":"A government still controls its territory but must negotiate financial rules with international institutions and multinational firms. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Sovereignty is constrained or negotiated, not abolished","The state is no longer sovereign in any sense","Globalization has ended","Only cultural globalization is occurring"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"Globalization changes how authority is exercised without erasing states."},{"q":"Which scenario best illustrates non-state political power under globalization?","opts":["An NGO and multinational firm shape negotiations across several governments","A parliament passes a purely local zoning rule","A village elects a headman","A court interprets a domestic traffic law"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"Cross-border non-state influence is central to the lecture’s changing-actor framework."},{"q":"An environmental campaign coordinates activists across five countries and pressures a trade institution. Which effect is most visible?","opts":["Transnational civil-society networks influence global governance","Industrial deglobalization","Purely domestic sovereignty","A return to imperial rule"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"NGOs/social movements can operate across borders and affect rule-making."},{"q":"Why are institutions such as the WTO, IMF and UN described as global governance rather than a world government?","opts":["They coordinate rules and cooperation without replacing sovereign states with one global sovereign","They possess unlimited authority over every domestic issue","They abolish national law automatically","They are all military alliances"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"Global governance is rule coordination across states, not a single world state."},{"q":"Why can globalization itself contribute to challenges against the liberal order?","opts":["Integration can help rising economies accumulate capabilities and alter relative power","Trade always freezes the power hierarchy","Economic growth cannot affect military capability","Only culture influences great-power status"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"Economic rise can translate into political and military power."},{"q":"A rising state gains economic and military power but accepts all existing rules and remains satisfied. What important risk condition is missing from the lecture’s power-transition story?","opts":["Dissatisfaction with the existing order","Population growth","Membership in a regional organization","Access to the internet"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"A challenge is especially expected when rising capability combines with dissatisfaction."},{"q":"Why does the hierarchy matter for power transition?","opts":["States’ relative positions and movement up the power ladder affect rivalry with the dominant power","It determines their official language","It removes all domestic politics","It makes trade unnecessary"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Power transition focuses on relative capability and status."},{"q":"Why is China’s rise a bridge between Lecture 2 and Lecture 3?","opts":["It turns globalization’s economic power shifts into a strategic competition problem for ASEAN","It proves ASEAN is irrelevant","It concerns only cultural globalization","It ends the need for hedging"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"Lecture 3 focuses on how ASEAN manages U.S.–China rivalry produced by shifting power."},{"q":"Why can U.S. primacy support globalization and simultaneously generate rivalry?","opts":["It provides order and open rules, but rising powers may resist a system centered on U.S. leadership","Primacy eliminates changes in relative power","Open rules prevent any strategic disagreement","Rivalry requires complete economic isolation"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"The same order can enable growth that later shifts power and creates contestation."},{"q":"Two rival powers trade heavily but also restrict strategic technology and strengthen militaries. Which conclusion best fits the course?","opts":["Economic interdependence and strategic rivalry can coexist","Trade proves their security competition is imaginary","The relationship is fully deglobalized","Military policy is unrelated to economic links"],"ans":0,"topic":"Post-Cold War international system and liberal international order","why":"Interdependence does not automatically neutralize security competition."}],"tu2-benefits":[{"q":"Country A is less efficient than B at producing both goods but gives up relatively less of one good. Why can trade still help?","opts":["Comparative advantage depends on relative opportunity cost, not being absolutely best at everything","Trade only helps the absolutely most productive country","The weaker country must stop producing","Comparative advantage requires identical opportunity costs"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Comparative advantage supports specialization based on relative costs."},{"q":"Why can global supply chains lower final-product costs?","opts":["Production stages can be located where firms have specialized cost or capability advantages","Every stage must be duplicated domestically","Cross-border production always raises costs","Supply chains eliminate transport needs"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Specialization across locations can improve efficiency."},{"q":"Why does global poverty reduction not refute criticism of inequality within countries?","opts":["Global averages can improve while gains are distributed unevenly among groups inside a country","Poverty and inequality are identical measures","If poverty falls, no worker can lose","Within-country distribution is irrelevant"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Global gains and domestic distributional losses can coexist."},{"q":"A technology invented in one country is quickly adapted in many others. Which mechanism is illustrated?","opts":["Cross-border diffusion of innovation","Cultural isolation","Trade reversal","Sovereignty elimination"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Global networks accelerate the spread and adaptation of technology."},{"q":"Why can gross trade grow faster than GDP when supply chains fragment production internationally?","opts":["Intermediate components may cross borders multiple times and be counted in gross trade at several stages","GDP counts every border crossing twice by definition","Trade statistics exclude intermediate goods","Supply chains prevent value added from being measured"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading notes gross trade can count repeated border crossings while GDP counts value added."},{"q":"Why is FDI deeper than simply importing a product?","opts":["It involves ownership or management of productive assets across countries","It is only a tariff payment","It is identical to tourism","It requires no cross-border capital commitment"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"FDI links firms to production assets in other economies."},{"q":"Why does falling tariff protection not mean trade is frictionless?","opts":["Transport, regulation, customs, standards and other non-tariff costs still matter","Tariffs are the only possible trade cost","Digital trade has no regulation","All domestic distribution costs disappear"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The reading distinguishes many forms of trade cost beyond tariffs."},{"q":"Why are NTMs politically ambiguous?","opts":["They can pursue legitimate public goals but can also be used protectionistically","They are always illegal","They always increase free trade","They cannot affect foreign firms"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The same measure can protect health/safety or shield domestic producers."},{"q":"Which change best illustrates the 'death of distance' for services?","opts":["A professional service can be delivered digitally across borders without physical shipment","A factory must move next to every customer","A tariff is imposed on steel","A country bans broadband"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Digital communication lets previously local services cross borders."},{"q":"How can this shift alter global politics as well as economics?","opts":["Greater economic weight can increase the political influence and bargaining power of rising states","Trade shares have no relation to power resources","Industrialization makes states less capable","Economic growth removes foreign-policy interests"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The lecture links economic power shifts to challenges in the international order."}],"tu2-challenges":[{"q":"A country gains overall from trade, but one region loses factories and wages stagnate. What does this illustrate?","opts":["Aggregate gains can coexist with concentrated domestic adjustment costs","Comparative advantage guarantees no losers","The country cannot have gained overall","This is proof that trade never affects labor markets"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Distribution matters separately from total gains."},{"q":"Why can intense global cost competition create labor-rights pressure?","opts":["Firms and suppliers may seek lower costs where workers have weaker bargaining power or protections","Competition always raises every labor standard automatically","Labor conditions are unrelated to production location","Only consumers determine workplace law"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Cost competition can encourage a race toward weaker protections without countervailing rules."},{"q":"A polluting industry relocates to a country with weaker environmental regulation. Which criticism is most direct?","opts":["Environmental costs can be displaced across borders rather than eliminated","Comparative advantage removes pollution","Globalization guarantees identical regulation","The issue is only cultural homogenization"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Cross-border production can shift environmental burdens."},{"q":"A local tradition survives but is redesigned for a global platform. Why is this more nuanced than simple homogenization?","opts":["Globalization can both standardize culture and generate localized hybrid adaptation","Any global influence erases local culture completely","Cultural change has no relation to globalization","Localization means deglobalization"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Global/local interaction can produce adaptation rather than total replacement."},{"q":"Why can an internationally negotiated rule be efficient yet still face legitimacy criticism?","opts":["Problem-solving capacity and democratic accountability are different dimensions","Efficiency automatically creates democratic consent","International rules cannot solve collective problems","Legitimacy only depends on economic growth"],"ans":0,"topic":"Global actors, governance, sovereignty, and power shifts","why":"A rule can work technically while citizens question who had authority to make it."},{"q":"A leader blames trade agreements and foreign competition for local job losses and promises tariffs. Which chain fits the lecture?","opts":["Distributional grievance → populist backlash → deglobalization pressure","Technology → full integration → no politics","Poverty reduction → automatic open borders","Regionalism → abolition of elections"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Economic losers can become a political constituency for protection."},{"q":"Why did the 2008–09 trade collapse matter for interpreting long-run globalization trends?","opts":["Large shocks can temporarily distort trade growth and reveal the vulnerability of integrated systems","It proved world trade had permanently ended","It showed GDP never changes during crises","It made political shocks irrelevant"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The WTO reading warns crisis effects can dominate short-period statistics."},{"q":"A government moves semiconductor supply away from a strategic rival despite higher cost. What logic dominates?","opts":["Security resilience is being prioritized over maximum economic efficiency","Comparative advantage has been mathematically disproved","Cultural homogenization is the main concern","The state is abandoning all international trade"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Strategic sectors may trade some efficiency for reduced vulnerability."},{"q":"A country remains open to tourism and consumer trade but restricts sensitive technology and reshapes supply chains. Is this compatible with deglobalization?","opts":["Yes; deglobalization can be selective and sector-specific","No; any cross-border activity means globalization can never decline","No; only total autarky counts","Yes, but only if the country leaves every institution"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"Strategic decoupling can coexist with continued integration elsewhere."},{"q":"Which policy approach best reflects the lecture’s final balance-sheet logic?","opts":["Pair openness with adjustment support, labor/environment rules and accountable governance","Ignore distribution because total GDP is all that matters","Ban all imports regardless of sector","Assume markets automatically solve every political legitimacy problem"],"ans":0,"topic":"Economic gains, inequality, populism, and deglobalization","why":"The lecture asks how to manage—not deny—the coexistence of benefits and costs."}]},"cumulativeId":"tu2-all","flashcards":{"id":"tu101-l2","label":"Lecture 2 • What is Globalization? Understanding the International System in an Era of Global Disruption","description":"Globalization, post-Cold War order, political/economic effects, criticism and deglobalization.","cumulativeId":"tu101-l2-full-v1","cumulativeLabel":"Full Lecture • Lecture 2","parts":[{"id":"tu2-l2-foundations-v1","label":"Part 1 • Meaning, Waves & Post-Cold War Order","description":"Definition, historical waves and the international order behind modern globalization.","cards":[{"id":"definition","front":"Which definition best matches globalization in the lecture?","back":"Growing interdependence of economies, cultures and populations through cross-border flows ไทยช่วยจำ: การพึ่งพากันข้ามพรมแดน ไม่ใช่แค่การค้า"},{"id":"dimensions","front":"Which set contains the four globalization dimensions emphasized in the slide?","back":"Economic, political, cultural and technological ไทยช่วยจำ: 4 มิติ: เศรษฐกิจ การเมือง วัฒนธรรม เทคโนโลยี"},{"id":"waves","front":"What is the correct broad sequence of globalization waves in the slide?","back":"Ancient trade → industrial wave → 1914–45 reversal → post-war → digital era ไทยช่วยจำ: คลื่น globalization มีทั้งขยายและถอยกลับ"},{"id":"industrial","front":"Why is the Industrial Revolution important to the WTO reading on trade?","back":"Transport and communication advances sharply reduced the costs of connecting markets ไทยช่วยจำ: เทคโนโลยีลดต้นทุนของระยะทาง"},{"id":"postcw","front":"What major structural change followed the Cold War in the lecture?","back":"The United States emerged as the sole superpower after bloc confrontation ended ไทยช่วยจำ: หลัง Cold War สหรัฐฯ เด่นและระเบียบเสรีเอื้อ globalization"},{"id":"lio","front":"What does Liberal International Order refer to in this lecture context?","back":"A U.S.-supported order emphasizing liberal rules, institutions and open economic relations ไทยช่วยจำ: LIO = ระเบียบกติกาเสรีที่สหรัฐฯ สนับสนุน"},{"id":"regionalism","front":"What regional trend accompanied post-Cold War globalization?","back":"Regionalism and regional integration expanded ไทยช่วยจำ: regional integration ช่วยจัดระเบียบการเชื่อมข้ามพรมแดน"},{"id":"technology","front":"Which is one of the three acceleration drivers named by the professor?","back":"Technology that enables real-time cross-border coordination ไทยช่วยจำ: เทคโนโลยีอย่างเดียวไม่พอ นโยบายมีผล"},{"id":"policy","front":"What policy choice helped accelerate globalization?","back":"Lowering trade barriers and signing agreements ไทยช่วยจำ: นโยบายเปิดเสรีเป็นตัวขับสำคัญ"},{"id":"ideology","front":"What ideological belief supported globalization in the lecture?","back":"Free markets were expected to support prosperity and peace ไทยช่วยจำ: อุดมการณ์กำหนดว่านโยบายแบบไหนถูกมองว่าน่าทำ"}]},{"id":"tu2-l2-politics-v1","label":"Part 2 • Sovereignty, Actors, Governance & Power","description":"Political consequences of transnational networks and changing power distribution.","cards":[{"id":"sovereignty","front":"How does globalization challenge nation-state sovereignty in the slide?","back":"Important decisions increasingly involve transnational actors and negotiated rules beyond borders ไทยช่วยจำ: อธิปไตยถูกท้าทาย/เจรจา ไม่ได้หายไป"},{"id":"actors","front":"Which is a new global actor emphasized by the lecture?","back":"Multinational corporations ไทยช่วยจำ: MNC/NGO มีอิทธิพลข้ามรัฐ"},{"id":"ngo","front":"Why are NGOs important in the globalization lecture?","back":"They can participate in transnational advocacy and governance alongside states ไทยช่วยจำ: NGO เชื่อม civil society ข้ามพรมแดน"},{"id":"institutions","front":"Which organization is associated with global trade rules in the slide?","back":"WTO ไทยช่วยจำ: global governance ≠ world government"},{"id":"power-shift","front":"What power shift does the slide emphasize?","back":"The relative economic weight of the U.S./Europe declined while China and other powers rose ไทยช่วยจำ: globalization เปลี่ยนดุลอำนาจได้"},{"id":"ptt","front":"What is the core claim of Power Transition Theory in the slide?","back":"A rising great power may challenge a dominant power, creating systemic tension ไทยช่วยจำ: กำลังเพิ่ม + ไม่พอใจ status quo = เสี่ยงปะทะ"},{"id":"hierarchy","front":"Which hierarchy appears in the Power Transition Theory slide?","back":"Dominant, great, middle and small powers ไทยช่วยจำ: ลำดับชั้นอำนาจใช้ดูการขยับขึ้นและการท้าทาย"},{"id":"china-rise","front":"What U.S. concern about China is highlighted in the slide?","back":"Economic growth can support military modernization and demands to revise rules ไทยช่วยจำ: จีนเติบโตทางเศรษฐกิจ → อำนาจยุทธศาสตร์ → ASEAN ต้องรับมือ"},{"id":"us-strategy","front":"What was a core aim of U.S. post-Cold War grand strategy described in the slide?","back":"Preserve U.S. leading status and prevent hostile domination of key regions ไทยช่วยจำ: ระเบียบที่สหรัฐฯ นำเอื้อ integration แต่ก็อาจถูกท้าทาย"},{"id":"interdependence","front":"Does economic interdependence automatically eliminate geopolitical competition in the lecture?","back":"No; integration can coexist with power rivalry and security concerns ไทยช่วยจำ: พึ่งพาทางเศรษฐกิจได้พร้อมกับแข่งขันด้านความมั่นคง"}]},{"id":"tu2-l2-benefits-v1","label":"Part 3 • Economic Benefits & Global Production","description":"Comparative advantage, poverty reduction, innovation, FDI and supply chains.","cards":[{"id":"comp-adv","front":"What economic idea explains gains from specialization and trade in the slide?","back":"Comparative advantage ไทยช่วยจำ: เปรียบเทียบต้นทุนเสียโอกาส ไม่ใช่ต้องเก่งที่สุดทุกอย่าง"},{"id":"prices","front":"What consumer benefit does the lecture associate with global competition?","back":"Lower prices and greater variety ไทยช่วยจำ: แบ่งขั้นตอนตามจุดแข็งช่วยลดต้นทุน"},{"id":"poverty","front":"Which broad global outcome is presented as a benefit of globalization?","back":"Large reductions in extreme poverty, especially in parts of Asia ไทยช่วยจำ: ความยากจนโลกลดได้ แต่ความเหลื่อมล้ำในประเทศเพิ่มได้"},{"id":"innovation","front":"How can globalization spread innovation?","back":"Ideas and technologies diffuse rapidly through cross-border networks and markets ไทยช่วยจำ: นวัตกรรมกระจายเร็วผ่านเครือข่ายโลก"},{"id":"supply-chain","front":"What is an integrated global supply chain?","back":"Production stages for one final product are distributed across multiple countries ไทยช่วยจำ: ชิ้นส่วนข้ามพรมแดนหลายรอบทำให้ gross trade โตเร็ว"},{"id":"fdi","front":"What post-1945 development did the WTO reading emphasize alongside trade growth?","back":"Rapid expansion of foreign direct investment and multinational production ไทยช่วยจำ: FDI = ลงทุน/ถือสินทรัพย์การผลิตข้ามประเทศ"},{"id":"tariffs","front":"What happened to many traditional tariffs during modern trade liberalization?","back":"Average tariff barriers generally fell through unilateral and negotiated opening ไทยช่วยจำ: ภาษีลด แต่ต้นทุน/กฎอื่นยังอยู่"},{"id":"ntm","front":"What is a non-tariff measure (NTM)?","back":"A non-tariff rule or policy that affects trade, such as technical or health standards ไทยช่วยจำ: NTM อาจปกป้องสาธารณะหรือใช้กีดกันก็ได้"},{"id":"services","front":"What made more services internationally tradable?","back":"Sharp reductions in communication costs and digital technologies ไทยช่วยจำ: บริการข้ามพรมแดนได้เพราะดิจิทัลลดความสำคัญของระยะทาง"},{"id":"developing","front":"What major change occurred in developing economies’ role in world trade?","back":"Their share of world trade rose substantially, especially with Asian industrialization ไทยช่วยจำ: น้ำหนักเศรษฐกิจเพิ่ม → อำนาจต่อรอง/การเมืองเพิ่มได้"}]},{"id":"tu2-l2-challenges-v1","label":"Part 4 • Inequality, Criticism & Deglobalization","description":"Distributional conflict, rights, environment, culture, populism and strategic fragmentation.","cards":[{"id":"inequality","front":"What inequality criticism does the lecture emphasize?","back":"Globalization can increase inequality within countries even while producing aggregate gains ไทยช่วยจำ: ภาพรวมได้ แต่บางพื้นที่/แรงงานเสีย"},{"id":"workers","front":"Which labor concern is listed among globalization’s criticisms?","back":"Low wages, unsafe conditions and weak bargaining protections in some supply chains ไทยช่วยจำ: แรงกดต้นทุนอาจทำให้แรงงานในจุดอ่อนถูกกด"},{"id":"environment","front":"Which environmental cost is associated with globalization in the slide?","back":"Transport emissions, pollution relocation, fast consumption and e-waste ไทยช่วยจำ: มลพิษอาจย้ายที่ ไม่ได้หายไป"},{"id":"culture","front":"What is cultural homogenization?","back":"Local differences can be weakened as global brands/languages/consumer patterns spread ไทยช่วยจำ: global อาจทำให้คล้ายกัน แต่ท้องถิ่นก็ปรับสร้างแบบผสมได้"},{"id":"dem-deficit","front":"What is the democratic-deficit criticism?","back":"Important rules may be shaped by institutions or negotiators with weak direct accountability to ordinary citizens ไทยช่วยจำ: ทำงานได้ดี ≠ ประชาชนรู้สึกว่ามีส่วนกำหนด"},{"id":"populism","front":"How can inequality contribute to populism in the deglobalization story?","back":"Groups who feel left behind may support leaders promising protection and national control ไทยช่วยจำ: ความรู้สึกถูกทิ้งไว้ข้างหลัง → populism → protectionism"},{"id":"crisis","front":"Why can economic crises slow globalization?","back":"Crises can increase protectionism, distrust and pressure to reduce external dependence ไทยช่วยจำ: วิกฤตทำให้การค้า/ความเชื่อมั่นหดและเห็นความเปราะบาง"},{"id":"geopolitics","front":"How can geopolitics drive deglobalization?","back":"Security rivalry can motivate decoupling, restrictions and supply-chain diversification ไทยช่วยจำ: ความมั่นคงอาจมาก่อน efficiency ในสินค้ายุทธศาสตร์"},{"id":"deglobal","front":"What does deglobalization most accurately mean in this course context?","back":"A reduction or reorganization of cross-border integration, often selectively rather than total isolation ไทยช่วยจำ: deglobalization มักเลือกบางด้าน ไม่ใช่ปิดโลกทั้งหมด"},{"id":"balance","front":"What central policy challenge closes the globalization balance sheet?","back":"Keep genuine benefits while distributing them more fairly and protecting workers, environment and democracy ไทยช่วยจำ: รักษาผลดี พร้อมแก้การกระจายผลประโยชน์และความคุ้มครอง"}]}]}};window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("d-study-tu101-module-loaded",{detail:{id:"l2"}}));})();